@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Text Blocks in Java";
}

<div class="card border-0 shadow-sm">
    <div class="card-body p-4 p-lg-5">

        <div class="d-flex justify-content-between align-items-center mb-4">
            <div>
                <h1 class="fw-bold mb-2">Text Blocks in Java</h1>
                <p class="text-muted mb-0">
                    Learn how Java text blocks simplify multi-line strings and formatted text.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <hr class="mb-4" />

        <!-- Introduction -->
        <h2 class="fw-bold">1. What are Text Blocks?</h2>

        <p>
            A <strong>text block</strong> is a multi-line string literal introduced
            as a preview feature in Java 13 and standardized in Java 15.
        </p>

        <p>
            Text blocks make it easier to write multi-line text such as:
        </p>

        <ul>
            <li>HTML</li>
            <li>JSON</li>
            <li>SQL</li>
            <li>XML</li>
            <li>Formatted messages</li>
            <li>Multi-line documentation</li>
        </ul>

        <div class="alert alert-info">
            <strong>Key idea:</strong>
            Text blocks reduce the need for repeated quotation marks,
            newline escape sequences, and string concatenation.
        </div>

        <!-- Traditional -->
        <h2 class="fw-bold mt-5">2. Traditional Multi-Line String</h2>

        <p>
            Before text blocks, multi-line strings often required explicit
            newline characters and string concatenation.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block mb-4">
            <span class="code-lang-tag">Java</span>
            <pre class="mb-0"><code>String message =
    "Hello Java\n"
    + "Welcome to CIIT Institute\n"
    + "Learn modern Java features";</code></pre>
        </div>

        <!-- Text block -->
        <h2 class="fw-bold mt-5">3. Basic Text Block</h2>

        <p>
            A text block starts and ends with three double quotation marks.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block mb-4">
            <span class="code-lang-tag">Java</span>
            <pre class="mb-0"><code>String message = """
        Hello Java
        Welcome to CIIT Institute
        Learn modern Java features
        """;</code></pre>
        </div>

        <p>
            The text is much easier to read because the source code resembles
            the resulting multi-line string.
        </p>

        <!-- Syntax -->
        <h2 class="fw-bold mt-5">4. Text Block Syntax</h2>

        <p>
            The opening delimiter is normally followed by a line break.
            The closing delimiter ends the text block.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block mb-4">
            <span class="code-lang-tag">Java</span>
            <pre class="mb-0"><code>String text = """
        First line
        Second line
        Third line
        """;</code></pre>
        </div>

        <!-- Newline -->
        <h2 class="fw-bold mt-5">5. Newline Characters</h2>

        <p>
            Each line in a text block normally contributes a line terminator
            to the resulting string.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block mb-4">
            <span class="code-lang-tag">Java</span>
            <pre class="mb-0"><code>String text = """
        Java
        Spring
        Hibernate
        """;</code>

</pre>
        </div>

        <p>
            Conceptually, the resulting value contains:
        </p>

        <div class="code-block mb-4">
            <span class="code-lang-tag">Output</span>
            <pre class="mb-0"><code>Java
Spring
Hibernate</code></pre>
        </div>

        <!-- Indentation -->
        <h2 class="fw-bold mt-5">6. Automatic Indentation</h2>

        <p>
            Java processes incidental indentation in text blocks so that the
            indentation used only to format source code does not unnecessarily
            become part of the resulting string.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block mb-4">
            <span class="code-lang-tag">Java</span>
            <pre class="mb-0"><code>String text = """
        Java
        Spring
        SQL
        """;</code></pre>
        </div>

        <p>
            This allows developers to indent the text block together with
            the surrounding Java code while keeping the resulting content clean.
        </p>

        <!-- HTML -->
        <h2 class="fw-bold mt-5">7. Text Blocks with HTML</h2>

        <p>
            Text blocks are especially useful when embedding HTML.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block mb-4">
            <span class="code-lang-tag">Java</span>
            <pre class="mb-0"><code>String html = """
        &lt;html&gt;
            &lt;body&gt;
                &lt;h1&gt;CIIT 🌍👨‍🏫&lt;/h1&gt;
            &lt;/body&gt;
        &lt;/html&gt;
        """;</code></pre>
        </div>

        <div class="alert alert-success">
            <strong>Benefit:</strong>
            HTML structure remains readable without excessive escape sequences.
        </div>

        <!-- JSON -->
        <h2 class="fw-bold mt-5">8. Text Blocks with JSON</h2>

        <p>
            JSON data can also be represented cleanly with text blocks.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block mb-4">
            <span class="code-lang-tag">Java</span>
            <pre class="mb-0"><code>String json = """
        {
            "name": "Pradnya",
            "role": "Java Developer",
            "experience": "Fresher"
        }
        """;</code></pre>
        </div>

        <!-- SQL -->
        <h2 class="fw-bold mt-5">9. Text Blocks with SQL</h2>

        <p>
            SQL queries become easier to format and maintain.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block mb-4">
            <span class="code-lang-tag">Java</span>
            <pre class="mb-0"><code>String sql = """
        SELECT id, name, email
        FROM users
        WHERE active = true
        ORDER BY name
        """;</code></pre>
        </div>

        <!-- Escape -->
        <h2 class="fw-bold mt-5">10. Escape Sequences</h2>

        <p>
            Text blocks still support Java escape sequences when they are required.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block mb-4">
            <span class="code-lang-tag">Java</span>
            <pre class="mb-0"><code>String text = """
        First line
        Second line
        """;</code></pre>
        </div>

        <p>
            Common escape sequences include:
        </p>

        <ul>
            <li><code>\n</code> - New line</li>
            <li><code>\t</code> - Tab</li>
            <li><code>\"</code> - Double quote</li>
            <li><code>\\</code> - Backslash</li>
        </ul>

        <!-- Explicit quote -->
        <h2 class="fw-bold mt-5">11. Double Quotes Inside Text Blocks</h2>

        <p>
            Double quotes can usually be written directly inside a text block
            without escaping each quote.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block mb-4">
            <span class="code-lang-tag">Java</span>
            <pre class="mb-0"><code>String message = """
        He said "Hello Sam"
        and started learning.
        """;</code></pre>
        </div>

        <!-- String methods -->
        <h2 class="fw-bold mt-5">12. Text Blocks are Strings</h2>

        <p>
            A text block produces a normal Java <code>String</code>.
            Therefore, normal String methods can be used.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block mb-4">
            <span class="code-lang-tag">Java</span>
            <pre class="mb-0"><code>String text = """
        Java
        Spring
        """;

System.out.println(
    text.length()
);

System.out.println(
    text.contains("CIIT🌍❤️")
);

System.out.println(
    text.toUpperCase()
);</code></pre>
        </div>

        <p class="mt-3">
    Output:
</p>

<div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">
    <div class="code-lang-tag">
        Output
    </div>

    <pre class="mb-0"><code>13
false
JAVA
SPRING</code></pre>
</div>
        <!-- formatted -->
        <h2 class="fw-bold mt-5">13. Formatted Text Blocks</h2>

        <p>
            Text blocks can be combined with String formatting methods.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block mb-4">
            <span class="code-lang-tag">Java</span>
            <pre class="mb-0"><code>String name = "Samadhan";
int age = 22;

String profile = """
        Name: %s
        Age: %d
        """.formatted(name, age);

System.out.println(profile);</code></pre>
        </div>

        <p class="mt-3">
    Output:
</p>

<div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">
    <div class="code-lang-tag">
        Output
    </div>

    <pre class="mb-0"><code>Name: Samadhan
Age: 22</code></pre>
</div>
        <div class="alert alert-info">
            <strong>Modern Java:</strong>
            The <code>formatted()</code> method provides a convenient way
            to insert values into a text block.
        </div>

        <!-- String.format -->
        <h2 class="fw-bold mt-5">14. String.format with Text Blocks</h2>

        <p>
            You can also use <code>String.format()</code> with a text block.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block mb-4">
            <span class="code-lang-tag">Java</span>
            <pre class="mb-0"><code>String name = "CIIT Institute";

String message = String.format(
    """
    Learning language: %s
    """,
    name
);

System.out.println(message);</code></pre>
        </div>

        <p class="mt-3">
    Output:
</p>

<div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">
    <div class="code-lang-tag">
        Output
    </div>

    <pre class="mb-0"><code>Name: Samadhan
Age: 22</code></pre>
</div>
        <!-- Incidental whitespace -->
        <h2 class="fw-bold mt-5">15. Incidental Whitespace</h2>

        <p>
            Text blocks distinguish between indentation introduced only by
            source formatting and whitespace that is intentionally part of
            the content.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block mb-4">
            <span class="code-lang-tag">Java</span>
            <pre class="mb-0"><code>String text = """
        Java
          Spring
            Boot
        """;</code></pre>
        </div>

        <p>
            Relative indentation inside the text can be preserved when it
            represents meaningful content.
        </p>

        <!-- Trailing spaces -->
        <h2 class="fw-bold mt-5">16. Trailing Spaces</h2>

        <p>
            Trailing whitespace can be controlled explicitly when necessary.
            Java provides the <code>\s</code> escape sequence for preserving
            trailing spaces at the end of a line.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block mb-4">
            <span class="code-lang-tag">Java</span>
            <pre class="mb-0"><code>String text = """
        Java\s
        Spring
        """;</code></pre>
        </div>

        <!-- Line continuation -->
        <h2 class="fw-bold mt-5">17. Line Continuation</h2>

        <p>
            The backslash escape can be used to prevent a source line break
            from becoming a line break in the resulting string.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block mb-4">
            <span class="code-lang-tag">Java</span>
            <pre class="mb-0"><code>String text = """
        Java \
        Spring \
        Boot
        """;</code></pre>
        </div>

        <p>
            This can be useful when source formatting should not introduce
            additional newline characters.
        </p>

        <!-- Text block conversion -->
        <h2 class="fw-bold mt-5">18. Converting Existing Strings</h2>

        <p>
            Text blocks are most useful when existing concatenated strings become
            difficult to read or maintain.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block mb-4">
            <span class="code-lang-tag">Before</span>
            <pre class="mb-0"><code>String html =
    "&lt;html&gt;\n"
    + "    &lt;body&gt;\n"
    + "        &lt;h1&gt;WelCome CIIT Training Institute🌍👀&lt;/h1&gt;\n"
    + "    &lt;/body&gt;\n"
    + "&lt;/html&gt;";</code></pre>
        </div>

        <div class="code-block mb-4">
            <span class="code-lang-tag">After</span>
            <pre class="mb-0"><code>String html = """
        &lt;html&gt;
            &lt;body&gt;
                &lt;h1&gt;WelCome CIIT Training Institute🌍👀&lt;/h1&gt;
            &lt;/body&gt;
        &lt;/html&gt;
        """;</code></pre>
        </div>

        <!-- Version -->
        <h2 class="fw-bold mt-5">19. Java Version</h2>

        <div class="table-responsive mb-4">
            <table class="table table-bordered">
                <thead class="table-dark">
                    <tr>
                        <th>Java Version</th>
                        <th>Text Blocks</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Java 13</td>
                        <td>Introduced as preview</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Java 14</td>
                        <td>Second preview</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Java 15</td>
                        <td>Standard feature</td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>

        <!-- Advantages -->
        <h2 class="fw-bold mt-5">20. Advantages of Text Blocks</h2>

        <ul>
            <li>Improves readability of multi-line strings.</li>
            <li>Reduces repeated string concatenation.</li>
            <li>Reduces unnecessary newline escape sequences.</li>
            <li>Makes HTML and JSON easier to maintain.</li>
            <li>Makes SQL queries easier to read.</li>
            <li>Works with normal String APIs.</li>
            <li>Supports String formatting.</li>
        </ul>

        <!-- Limitations -->
        <h2 class="fw-bold mt-5">21. Limitations</h2>

        <ul>
            <li>Requires Java 15 or later as a standard feature.</li>
            <li>Whitespace behavior should be understood when formatting important text.</li>
            <li>Text blocks are not a separate runtime data type; they produce Strings.</li>
            <li>Very short strings are usually clearer with ordinary string literals.</li>
        </ul>

        <!-- Real world -->
        <h2 class="fw-bold mt-5">22. Example 🌍🫠</h2>

        <p>
            Suppose a Java application needs to generate an email template.
            A text block makes the template much easier to maintain.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block mb-4">
            <span class="code-lang-tag">Java</span>
            <pre class="mb-0"><code>String customerName = "Samiksha";

String email = """
        Dear %s,

        Welcome to CIIT Institute.

        We are happy to have you
        learning Java with us.

        Regards,
        CIIT Institute
        """.formatted(customerName);

System.out.println(email);</code></pre>
        </div>
        <p class="mt-3">
    Output:
</p>

<div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">
    <div class="code-lang-tag">
        Output
    </div>

    <pre class="mb-0"><code>Dear Samiksha,

Welcome to CIIT Institute.

We are happy to have you
learning Java with us.

Regards,
CIIT Institute</code></pre>
</div>
        <!-- Best practices -->
        <h2 class="fw-bold mt-5">23. Best Practices</h2>

        <ul>
            <li>Use text blocks for genuinely multi-line content.</li>
            <li>Use normal strings for short single-line values.</li>
            <li>Keep indentation consistent.</li>
            <li>Use <code>formatted()</code> for readable value substitution.</li>
            <li>Be careful when whitespace has semantic meaning.</li>
            <li>Use the Java version required by the project.</li>
        </ul>

